.013 .001(  to construct the predictor, repeat the procedure and combine.   Bagging)J
90 121 :M
.417 .042(applied to CART gave dramatic decreases in test set errors.)J
90 145 :M
.699 .07(Freund and Schapire recently [1995], [1996] proposed a P&C algorithm which was designed to)J
90 157 :M
.791 .079(drive the training set error rapidly to zero.   But if their algorithm is run far past the  point at)J
90 169 :M
.742 .074(which the training set error is zero, it gives better performance than bagging  on  a number of)J
90 181 :M
.293 .029(real data sets.   The crux of their idea is this:  start with p\(n\) = 1/N and resample from T to form)J
90 195 :M
.356 .036(the first training set T)J
0 -3 rm
.085(\(1\).)A
0 3 rm
.348 .035(   As the sequence of classifiers and training sets is being built, increase)J
90 207 :M
1.397 .14(p\(n\) for those cases that have been most frequently missclassifed.  At termination, combine)J
90 219 :M
.598 .06(classifiers by weighted or simple voting.   We will refer to algorithms of this type as  Adaptive)J
90 231 :M
.159 .016(Resampling  and Combining, or arcing algorithms.  In honor of Freund and Schapire's discovery,)J
90 243 :M
1.921 .192(we denote their specific algorithm by arc-fs, and discuss their theoretical efforts to relate)J
90 255 :M
.216 .022(training set to test set error in  the Appendix .)J
90 279 :M
.347 .035(To better understand stability and instability, and  what bagging and arcing do,  in Section 2 we)J
90 291 :M
1.455 .146(define the concepts of bias and variance for classifiers.  The difference between the test set)J
90 303 :M
1.301 .13(misclassification error for the classifier and the minimum error achievable is the sum of the)J
90 315 :M
1.926 .193(bias and variance.  Unstable classifiers such as trees characteristically have high variance)J
90 327 :M
1.336 .134(and low bias.  Stable classifers like linear discriminant analysis have low  variance, but can)J
90 339 :M
1.08 .108(have high bias.   This is illustrated on several examples of artificial data.   Section 3 looks at)J
90 351 :M
.169 .017(the effects of arcing and bagging trees on bias and variance.)J
90 375 :M
.663 .066(The main effect of both bagging and arcing  is to reduce variance.  Arcing seems to usually do)J
90 387 :M
.46 .046(better at  this\312than bagging.    Arc-fs does complex things and its behavior is puzzling.   But the)J
90 399 :M
.625 .063(variance reduction comes from the adaptive resampling and not the specific form of arc-fs.  To)J
90 411 :M
.74 .074(show this, we define a simpler arc algorithm denoted by arc-x4 whose accuracy is comparable)J
90 423 :M
.436 .044(to arc-fs.  The two appear to be at opposite poles of the arc spectrum.  Arc-x4  was concocted to)J
90 435 :M
1.496 .15(demonstrate that arcing works not because of the specific form of the arc-fs algorithm, but)J
90 447 :M
.718 .072(because of the adaptive resampling.)J
90 471 :M
.72 .072(Freund and Schapire [1996] compare arc-fs to bagging on 27 data sets and conclude that arc-fs)J
90 483 :M
.33 .033(has a small edge in test set error rates.   We tested arc-fs, arc-x4 and bagging  on the 10 real data)J
90 495 :M
.995 .1(sets used in our bagging  paper and get results more favorable to arcing.  These are given in)J
90 507 :M
.486 .049(Section 4.  arc-fs and arc-x4 finish in a dead heat.   On a few data sets one or the other is a little)J
90 519 :M
1.119 .112(better, but both are almost always significantly better than bagging.   We also look at arcing)J
90 531 :M
.685 .069(and bagging applied to the US Postal Service digit data base.)J
90 555 :M
.619 .062(The overall results of arcing are exciting--it turns a good but not great classifier \(CART\)  into a)J
90 567 :M
2.572 .257(procedure that seems to usually get close to the lowest achievable test set error rates.)J
90 579 :M
.666 .067(Furthermore, the arc-classifier is off-the-shelf.  Its performance does not depend on any tuning)J
90 591 :M
.296 .03(or settings for particular problems.  Just read in the data and press the start button.  It is also, by)J
90 603 :M
.304 .03(neural net standards, blazingly fast to construct.)J
90 627 :M
.643 .064(Section 5  gives the results of some experiments aimed at understanding how arc-fs and arc-x4)J
90 639 :M
2.048 .205(work.   Each algorithm has distinctive and different signatures.  Generally, arc-fs uses a)J
90 651 :M
.372 .037(smaller number of distinct cases in the resampled training sets and the successive values of p\(n\))J
90 663 :M
.948 .095(are highly variable.  The successive training sets in arc-fs rock back and forth and there is no)J
90 675 :M
.525 .053(convergence to a final set of {p\(n\)}.  The back and forth rocking is more subdued in arc-x4 , but)J
90 687 :M
.54 .054(there is still no convergence to a final {p\(n\)}.   This variability may be an essential ingredient of)J
90 699 :M
.257 .026(successful arcing algorithms.)J
endp
