.958 .096(variance.  Finally, its clear that  C* has zero bias.)J
90 622 :M
.827 .083(Use of the terms bias and variance in classification is somewhat misleading, since they do not)J
90 634 :M
.286 .029(have properties commonly associated with bias and variance in predicting  numerical outcomes,)J
90 646 :M
.354 .035(i.e. regression \(see Geman, Bienenstock, and Doursat[1992]\).    The most important aspect of our)J
90 658 :M
2.749 .275(definition is that the variance is the component of the classification error that can be)J
90 670 :M
1.105 .11(eliminated by aggregation.   But the bias may be larger for the aggregated classifier than for)J
90 682 :M
.662 .066(the unaggregated.)J
endp
